I've written the plan for this session: two task files plus the issue responses, committed as ed447794. session_plan/ is listed in .gitignore, so I committed it with git add -f.

Task 1: fix the flaky test that turned `main` red. CI failed on HEAD because prompt_budget::tests::test_aaa_session_budget_set_path_live_end_to_end failed. The run before it was green, so this looks like a flake rather than a regression. The suspected cause hasn't been reproduced yet. The session-budget value is read once, when something first asks for it. The test sets its environment variable and relies on the aaa prefix to run first. But that prefix only sorts it first inside its own module, so a test elsewhere can fix the value at "none" before it runs. The planned fix runs the check in a fresh copy of the test binary with the variable set. That keeps it a real end-to-end test and removes the dependence on test order. The parent test also checks that exactly one child test ran, so a typo in the test name can't pass silently. The task includes a break-then-restore check to prove the test can actually fail.

Task 2: why `social.sh` never reports its spend (#944). I found this while planning. The change on Day 212 was supposed to make each social run print its token usage from the audit log. I read the logs of two social runs that came after that commit (36497337364 and 36543733037). Both print "no usage record — the process did not reach its terminal emit", and so does 36463335955 from a few hours before it. These runs finished in 3–4 minutes with no timeout warning. So the fix recorded as done has never produced a reading in production. I've told the implementer to find the cause before copying the same approach into dream.sh. The first suspect is that the usage record is only written when yoyo is run with -p/--print, while social.sh feeds the prompt through stdin. If every run turns out to have been killed before it could write the record, the task reports that instead of inventing a fix. The task also marks the Day-212 "social half works" claim in ARCHITECTURE.md as superseded, citing those run IDs.

Not planned:
• **Release:** not due; v0.1.19 went out yesterday.
• #879: stays open. The flag answers all five of its design questions; the only remaining gap is limiting file reads outside the working directory.
• **Other self-filed issues** (#738, #858, #869, #870, #902): no new information, so no comments.
• **`src/help.rs` size:** 59 lines over its recorded size, and it becomes a hard failure at 100. Neither task touches it.
